WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Thanks for tuning in to SpaceX Daily, Episode 17, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.960
It's June 29th, 2026.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.000
From the launch pad to the trading floor, here's everything that moved today.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.100
The 15,000-pound Sirius XM satellite launch on Falcon 9 demonstrates the vehicle-sustained

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:25.120
heavy payload cadence while Starship 40's static-fire keeps flight 13 hardware moving forward.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.120
SpaceX launched the 7.5 ton Sirius XM satellite from Florida aboard a Falcon 9.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.420
The spacecraft forms part of a constellation refresh and represents a single heavy commercial

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.420
payload. Its mass exceeds a typical Starlink stack by roughly a factor of six.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.800
The flight keeps Falcon 9 active on non-Starlink missions even as the vehicle maintains its

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.560
primary Starlink cadence. Reports indicate the same booster family handled the heavy lift

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.940
without any structural changes from its lighter payload configuration. While that heavy

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.820
lift flight shows payload flexibility, the same booster family also maintained high-volume

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:14.780
Starlink operations on both coasts. A Falcon 9 from California placed 24 Starlink satellites

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.800
into orbit. The booster returned for a landing and extended its reuse count.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.180
One day later, a second Falcon 9 lifted another 24 satellites from Vandenberg into Polar

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:32.480
orbit. That west coast launch added coverage density at higher latitudes. Both boosters were

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.220
recovered as planned and observers noted the back-to-back temple across separate coasts.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.340
The missions add directly to broadband capacity and support future direct-to-cell services

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.880
through onboard laser links that will mesh with the existing constellation. Several accounts track

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.980
the exact timing of the Vandenberg landing relative to the California flight, underscoring how

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:03.820
parallel operations sustain overall cadence. Discussion also centered on how the Sirius XM

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:10.280
payload mass compares with typical ride share stacks and what that implies for future non-Starlink

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:17.140
manifests. Unverified posts suggested the next Starlink group may test an updated laser terminal

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.880
variant. The same engineering margins that allow these quick-turned Starlink flights are now

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:30.080
being examined in detail. From an engineering standpoint, the 7.5-ton Sirius XM satellite

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.600
illustrates how Falcon 9's first-stage recovery architecture tolerates large deviations

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:42.340
in payload mass without redesign. Nine Merlin engines must throttle and gimbal across a wider

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:49.000
center of mass envelope than a 500kg Starlink stack. Yet the same propellant load and structural

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.360
margins suffice because the vehicle was originally sized for crew dragon missions rather than the

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:00.280
lightest possible satellite. That original design choice created excess performance margin that

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:06.580
now lets a single booster alternate between high-volume constellation replenishment and infrequent

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:12.580
heavy commercial flights without tooling changes or new structural elements. Consider the physics.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.740
The first stage must still reach the same velocity and altitude targets for recovery.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.780
Even when the upper stage carries six times the usual mass so the engines operate across a

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.540
broader throttle band while the interstage and faring loads remain within the same certified

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:34.360
envelope. The result is that turnaround intervals stay consistent because the recovery hardware,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.660
grid fins and landing legs experience comparable stresses regardless of payload weight.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.620
Next, engineers will watch whether the same stage can maintain its current reuse cadence after

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:51.580
carrying the heavier upper stage load because any added thermal or structural fatigue which show

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:57.360
up first on the post-flight inspections already tracked across the fleet. That hardware flexibility

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:04.440
now extends beyond launch vehicles. Elon Musk stated that SpaceX is assigning top starship

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:10.160
and starlink engineers to grok development. The move directly links propulsion and satellite

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:18.600
hardware teams to the XAI software effort. Open AI and Thropic and XAI together now account for

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:25.460
21% of global AI compute capacity. The figure underscores the concentrated demand these three

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:32.260
organizations place on both terrestrial and potentially orbital resources. On the AI front

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:38.260
the reassignment shows how orbital hardware expertise is feeding directly into large-scale AI

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:45.260
training infrastructure. Worth keeping an eye on, SPC-X closed at its lowest level since the June

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:52.120
12 IPO following the Starship 40 static fire report. The decline occurred despite ongoing

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:57.380
launch activity and reflects investor focus on capital requirements for the Starship program.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.540
Resolution would require either clearer timelines on revenue generating flights or demonstrated

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:09.540
progress on cost reduction milestones already outlined in the prospectus. The engineering cadence

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.760
continues across multiple programs even as the market absorbs the latest static fire data.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:24.680
And a quick market note, SPC-X is at $153.23 up 0.3% versus the previous close.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.160
Before we go, watch for whether the next starlink group flies with the updated laser terminal

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:36.820
variant observers flagged in the last 24 hours. That's your SpaceX news for today.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:48.420
SPC-X is trading at $153.23, up 0.3%. If the show saves you time, a rating or review on Apple

